steps.body:
A `for` header has three parts separated by semicolons: start, condition, and update.

```java
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    System.out.println(i); // 0 1 2 3 4
}
```

Java runs the start once. Before every pass it checks the condition: if it is true, the body runs and then the update. The first false check ends the loop. Starting at 0 with `i < n` runs exactly n times. Use `int i = 1; i <= n` when you want the numbers 1 to n themselves. Mixing the two, as in `i = 0; i <= n`, runs n + 1 times, a classic off-by-one bug.

`i` exists only inside the loop, so using it after the closing brace is a compile error. To keep a running total or count, declare it before the loop so it survives:

```java
int sum = 0;
for (int i = 1; i <= 4; i++) {
    sum += i;
}
System.out.println(sum); // 10
```

An `if`/`else` inside the body runs fresh for every value of `i`, so each number can land in a different branch.

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int total = 0;
        int evens = 0;
        for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
            if (i % 2 == 0) {
                System.out.println(i + " even");
                evens++;
            } else {
                System.out.println(i + " odd");
            }
            total += i;
        }
        System.out.println("Evens: " + evens);
        System.out.println("Total: " + total);
    }
}

