practice.solution:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int replaced = 0;
        for (int i = 1; i <= 15; i++) {
            if (i % 15 == 0) {
                System.out.println("FizzBuzz");
                replaced++;
            } else if (i % 3 == 0) {
                System.out.println("Fizz");
                replaced++;
            } else if (i % 5 == 0) {
                System.out.println("Buzz");
                replaced++;
            } else {
                System.out.println(i);
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Replaced: " + replaced);
    }
}

steps.body:
The update can be any statement that moves `i`. Count down with `i--` and flip the condition so the loop keeps going while `i` is still big enough:

```java
for (int i = 3; i >= 1; i--) {
    System.out.println(i); // 3 2 1
}
```

Step by more than one with `i += step` or `i -= step`:

```java
for (int i = 0; i <= 20; i += 5) {
    System.out.println(i); // 0 5 10 15 20
}
```

Watch three things. First, flip the condition along with the direction. A countdown from 10 with `i < 10` never runs, and one with `i <= 10` runs about two billion times, until `i` wraps around past `Integer.MIN_VALUE`. Second, the update must change `i`. Writing `i + 2` does not even compile, because it is not a statement: use `i += 2`. Third, decide whether the end value is included: `i >= 0` reaches 0, but `i > 0` stops at 1. With a step, the loop stops at the last value that still passes the condition, so `i += 3` from 0 with `i <= 20` ends at 18.

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (int fuel = 25; fuel <= 100; fuel += 25) {
            System.out.println("Fuel " + fuel + "%");
        }
        for (int i = 10; i >= 0; i -= 2) {
            System.out.println("T-" + i);
        }
        System.out.println("Liftoff!");
    }
}

