steps.body:
Put a loop inside a loop to work in rows and columns. The inner loop runs all the way through on every pass of the outer loop.

```java
for (int row = 1; row <= 2; row++) {
    for (int col = 1; col <= 3; col++) {
        System.out.print(row * col + " ");
    }
    System.out.println();
}
```

```text
1 2 3
2 4 6
```

Use `System.out.print` inside the inner loop so values stay on one line, and one `System.out.println()` after the inner loop to end the row. Put `println` inside the inner loop by mistake and you get one value per line.

The inner bound can use the outer variable. With `j <= i`, row 1 gets one item, row 2 gets two, and so on, which draws a triangle. Give each loop its own name such as `i` and `j`: declaring another `i` in the inner header is a compile error, because the outer `i` is still in scope.

Each row printed this way ends with a space. You cannot see it, and the checker ignores trailing spaces.

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (int i = 1; i <= 4; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= i; j++) {
                System.out.print(j + " ");
            }
            System.out.println();
        }
    }
}

