steps.body:
`break` leaves the loop right away. `continue` skips the rest of the current pass and jumps to the update, so the loop moves on to the next value.

```java
for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
    if (i % 2 == 0) {
        continue; // skip even numbers
    }
    if (i > 7) {
        break; // stop for good
    }
    System.out.print(i + " "); // 1 3 5 7
}
```

Both affect only the innermost loop they sit in. In nested loops a plain `break` in the inner loop just moves on to the next outer pass. To leave both loops at once, put a label before the outer loop and name it in the `break`:

```java
outer:
for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
    for (int j = 1; j <= 3; j++) {
        System.out.print("(" + i + "," + j + ") "); // (1,1) (1,2) (1,3)
        if (i + j == 4) {
            break outer;
        }
    }
}
```

`continue outer;` works the same way and jumps to the next pass of the outer loop. Labels are rare in everyday code, so save them for nested loops.

One gotcha: a `break` inside a `switch` case leaves only the `switch`, even when that switch sits inside a loop.

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (int i = 1; i <= 20; i++) {
            if (i % 3 == 0) {
                continue;
            }
            if (i > 15) {
                break;
            }
            System.out.print(i + " ");
        }
        System.out.println();

        outer:
        for (int i = 1; i <= 9; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= 9; j++) {
                if (i * j == 24) {
                    System.out.println(i + " * " + j + " = 24");
                    break outer;
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

